---

## Observations — Haiku 4.5 vs Opus 4.8

On this small, clean, fully in-context table the two models were **effectively
identical**: both went 10/10 on the factual/trap set and both produced matching,
validated judgment answers. Both caught the goals-reconciliation trap (A10) and
respected the shot-volume floor (B4). Haiku even volunteered the full top-5
orderings for B2–B4 unprompted.

The takeaway mirrors the Opus run: for lookup and well-specified computation over
a tiny clean dataset, even the smaller/faster Claude model is reliable. This is
the regime LLMs handle best, so the parity is expected rather than surprising —
the discriminating tests would be a larger/messier context, undefined metrics,
or a weaker non-Claude model, which is where divergence and confident errors are
more likely to appear.
